<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sutan Sjahrir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1909–1966</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sutan Sjahrir was an Indonesian nationalist and socialist leader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a>. Exiled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boven-digoel">Boven-Digoel</a> and Banda Neira in the 1930s, he refused to collaborate with the Japanese during the occupation. As the first prime minister of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, from 1945 to 1947, he led negotiations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linggadjati-agreement">Linggadjati Agreement</a>. Later imprisoned under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, he died in Zürich.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir.jpg" alt="Sutan Sjahrir" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Written by St. Rais Alamsjah, published by Mutiara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutan_Sjahrir,_10_Orang_Indonesia_Terbesar_Sekarang_(1952),_p136.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
